== 1 Introduction <introduction>

Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space and let $cal(B) \( H \)$ denote its bounded linear operators.
  The #emph[commutant] of $S in cal(B) \( H \)$ is

$ { S }' = { A in cal(B) \( H \) : A S = S A } . $

A closed subspace is #emph[hyperinvariant] for $S$ if every operator in ${ S }'$ leaves it invariant.
  An operator algebra is #emph[transitive] if its only closed common invariant subspaces are ${ 0 }$ and $H$.
  Thus $S$ has no nonzero proper closed hyperinvariant subspace precisely when its commutant is transitive.

The hyperinvariant-subspace problem asks whether every bounded nonscalar operator on an infinite-dimensional
  separable complex Hilbert space has a nonzero proper closed hyperinvariant subspace.
  The construction below gives a negative answer. Its purpose is to exhibit the mechanism explicitly:
  the ordinary invariant subspaces of each weighted-shift fiber are coordinate tails, and a second,
  commuting operator moves those tails backward. Invariance under the commutant therefore imposes a strict descent
  that cannot persist on a probability space.

=== 1.1 The construction theorem <the-construction-theorem>

Put $K = ell^2 \( bb(Z) \)$, with orthonormal basis $\( e_j \)_(j in bb(Z))$, and define

$ K_(gt.eq h) = overline("span") { e_j : j gt.eq h } quad \( h in bb(Z) \) \, wide K_(gt.eq - oo) = K \, wide
  K_(gt.eq + oo) = { 0 } . $

Let $X = bb(Z)_2$ be the compact additive group of $2$-adic integers,
  the inverse limit of the groups $bb(Z) \/ 2^m bb(Z)$. Equip it with completed Haar probability $mu$,
  put $theta x = x + 1$, and set $H_0 = L^2 \( X \, mu \; K \)$.
  For $g in L^oo \( X \)$, the scalar multiplier is $\( M_g f \) \( x \) = g \( x \) f \( x \)$.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[There are measurable positive weights $beta_j : X arrow.r \( 0 \, oo \)$, $j in bb(Z)$,
    and a contraction $V in cal(B) \( H_0 \)$ with the following properties. The shifts
$ S_x e_j = beta_j \( x \) e_(j + 1) \, wide \( S f \) \( x \) = S_x f \( x \) \, $

 define a bounded operator $S$ on $H_0$, and:]
  + #emph[For every $x in X$, the closed invariant subspaces of $S_x$ are exactly $K_(gt.eq h)$,
    $h in bb(Z) union { - oo \, + oo }$.]
  + #emph[The operator $S$ is nonzero, $parallel S parallel = e^(- 10)$, and for every integer $n gt.eq 1$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1)")
 $ parallel S^n parallel lt.eq exp (- 10 ⌊frac(\( n + 1 \)^2, 4)⌋) . $ <eq:main-power-bound>]

]
  + #emph[The operator $V$ commutes with $S$ and has the form
$ \( V f \) \( theta x \) = V_x f \( x \) \, wide V_x e_j = b_j \( x \) e_(j - 1) \, $

 where $0 lt.eq b_j \( x \) lt.eq 1$ are measurable and are positive on a common conull set. On that set,
$ overline(V_x K_(gt.eq h)) = K_(gt.eq h - 1) quad \( h in bb(Z) \) \, wide overline(V_x K) = K . $

]
  + #emph[The unital complex algebra generated by $S$, $V$, and all scalar multipliers $M_g$ is transitive.]
] <thm:direct-model>

All generators in the last assertion commute with $S$. The explicit power bound implies norm-quasinilpotence,
  $lim_(n arrow.r oo) parallel S^n parallel^(1 \/ n) = 0$, while the first assertion will give many ordinary
  invariant subspaces of the resulting operator.

#thm([Corollary 1.2.])[
  #emph[Every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space admits a nonzero bounded norm-quasinilpotent
    operator with no nonzero proper closed hyperinvariant subspace.
    Its commutant is a proper strongly closed unital transitive complex operator algebra.]
] <cor:direct-algebra>

Here the strong operator topology is pointwise norm convergence on $H$.
  Consequently the commutant in the corollary also gives a negative answer to the question whether every unital
  transitive operator algebra is strongly dense in $cal(B) \( H \)$.

=== 1.2 Context and relation to the companion <context-and-relation-to-the-companion>

Douglas and Pearcy studied the connection between hyperinvariant subspaces and transitive
  algebras #cite(label("DouglasPearcy1972")). A major positive result is Arveson’s density theorem:
  a transitive algebra on a separable Hilbert space which contains a maximal abelian self-adjoint subalgebra of
  $cal(B) \( H \)$ is strongly dense in $cal(B) \( H \)$ #cite(label("Arveson1967")) Theorem 3.3.
  The scalar multiplier algebra used here has infinite multiplicity.
  Section 6 gives an explicit operator commuting with every such multiplier that is not itself a scalar
  multiplier, so the maximality hypothesis is absent.

Domar’s work on invariant subspaces of weighted translation spaces #cite(label("Domar1981")) and Grabiner’s work
  on unicellular shifts #cite(label("Grabiner1982")) provide the classical lattice ideas behind the fibers.
  Section 2 proves the precise quadratic weighted-translation criterion we need.
  Its convex-envelope and support-separation argument follows Domar’s method.
  The proof includes both normalized convolution-tail estimates;
  no general criterion for arbitrary weights is assumed. Our additional task is to link the fibers by a bounded
  commuting operator that moves their invariant tails backward.
  Opposite logarithmic changes at the two ends of the bilateral sequence make this possible,
  and one measurable normalization controls the operator over the whole base.

Atzmon and Sodin constructed completely indecomposable bilateral weighted shifts with spectrum the unit
  circle #cite(label("AtzmonSodin1999")); their 1999 paper left open whether those shifts have proper
  hyperinvariant subspaces. Here the extra commutant action is supplied by transport between different fibers.
  For a study of operators with shift-type invariant subspaces, see Kérchy #cite(label("Kerchy2026")).
  The invariant- and hyperinvariant-subspace problems must remain distinct:
  the Kérchy–Pearcy preprint claiming an equivalence was withdrawn after a counterexample to its Theorem 3
  #cite(label("KerchyPearcy2025Withdrawn")). The operator constructed here preserves every fixed tail
  $L^2 \( X \; K_(gt.eq h) \)$; its hyperinvariant conclusion is proved directly.

The companion paper #cite(label("CircleCompanion")) constructs, for every prescribed irrational rotation,
  a continuous circle weight whose operator in the rotation factor has no nontrivial invariant projection in that
  factor. It derives the existence conclusion of Corollary 2 by the usual projection argument;
  see also Dykema #cite(label("Dykema2005")) Proposition 2.1. The present construction has a different purpose:
  it exhibits the ordinary invariant tails, the bounded operator that moves them backward,
  and the explicit estimate (1). The two proofs use different constructions.
  Our only proof imports from the companion are the two elementary facts stated below.

=== 1.3 Proof overview and elementary inputs <proof-overview-and-elementary-inputs>

The proof first makes the possible fiber subspaces completely explicit.
  Section 2 classifies invariant subspaces of a weighted translation whose logarithmic weights have opposite
  quadratic growth at the two ends. Section 3 constructs weights over $bb(Z)_2$ satisfying that criterion in every
  fiber. Their decay also gives the uniform power estimate,
  which proves norm-quasinilpotence of the operator on $H_0$ rather than merely of its individual fibers.

To couple the fibers, we prescribe a map from $e_j$ at $x$ to a positive multiple of $e_(j - 1)$ at $theta x$.
  Commutation with the forward shifts determines a recurrence for those multiples.
  Section 4 solves the recurrence in both directions. The logarithmic changes of the weights under $theta$ have
  opposite signs at the two ends; the coefficients therefore decay in both directions.
  Dividing all coefficients in each fiber by their common supremum yields the contraction $V$ without changing
  commutation.

Finally, a subspace invariant under every scalar multiplier has an orthogonal projection represented by a
  measurable projection field. Invariance under $S$ makes each fiber a tail $K_(gt.eq s \( x \))$.
  Section 5 uses the dense images under $V_x$ to obtain

$ s \( theta x \) lt.eq s \( x \) - 1 wide upright("whenever ") s \( x \) in bb(Z) . $

Probability preservation excludes finite labels, and ergodicity excludes a nonconstant mixture of full and zero
  fibers. Section 6 then proves the corollary and records the structure of the example.

We use the following two elementary inputs, with the stated scope.
  Their complete proofs are in the companion #cite(label("CircleCompanion")).

+ #emph[Fields from scalar multipliers] #cite(label("CircleCompanion")) Lemma 3.1.
  For a probability space $\( Y \, nu \)$, $K = ell^2 \( bb(Z) \)$,
  and $A in cal(B) \( L^2 \( Y \; K \) \)$ commuting with every scalar multiplier,
  there is an essentially bounded field $A_y in cal(B) \( K \)$, unique almost everywhere, such that
  $ \( A f \) \( y \) = A_y f \( y \) \, wide parallel A_y parallel lt.eq parallel A parallel quad upright("a.e.") $
  For every $v in K$, the map $y mapsto A_y v$ is measurable.
    Products and adjoints act fiberwise; an orthogonal projection has orthogonal projection fibers outside one null set.
+ #emph[The commutant algebra] #cite(label("CircleCompanion")) Lemma 1.2.
  For any bounded nonscalar operator on a complex Hilbert space,
  its commutant is a proper strongly closed unital complex algebra.
  If its only closed common invariant subspaces are zero and the whole space, that algebra is transitive.

The first input requires no base transformation, covariance, trace or factor assumption.
  We apply it only after proving reduction by all scalar multipliers.
  The second requires neither separability nor quasinilpotence;
  the constructed operator will satisfy its nonscalarity hypothesis.
  The field input is used in Section 5, and the commutant input in Section 6.

== 2 The quadratic weighted-translation criterion <sec:criterion>

We first prove a criterion that makes every invariant fiber subspace a coordinate tail.
  The proof concerns one weighted sequence and does not involve the base transformation.

For a positive sequence $w = \( w_j \)_(j in bb(Z))$, set

$ ell^2 \( w \) = {z : sum_(j in bb(Z)) \| w_j z_j \|^2 < oo} \, wide
  lr(bar.v.double z bar.v.double)_(ell^2 \( w \))^2 = sum_(j in bb(Z)) \| w_j z_j \|^2 . $

If $w$ is decreasing, right translation $\( R z \)_j = z_(j - 1)$ is a contraction on this space.
  For a sequence $q_j$ on an integer interval, convexity means that $q_(j + 1) -
  q_j$ is nondecreasing wherever the differences are defined; concavity means the reverse.

#thm([Lemma 2.1.])[
  (Weighted translation criterion).  #emph[Let $w = \( w_j \)_(j in bb(Z))$ be positive and decreasing.
    Suppose that $log w_j$ is convex for $j lt.eq 0$ and concave for $j gt.eq 0$, that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2)")
 $ sum_(j eq.not 0) w_j^(1 \/ j) < oo \, $ <eq:root-sum>]

 and that, for some $a > log 3$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3)")
 $ lim_(n arrow.r oo) frac(log w_n, n^2) = - a \, wide lim_(n arrow.r oo) frac(log w_(- n), n^2) = a . $ <eq:quadratic>]

 Then the closed $R$-invariant subspaces of $ell^2 \( w \)$ are precisely
$ { z : z_j = 0 upright(" for ") j < h } \, wide h in bb(Z) union { - oo \, + oo } . $

 Consequently the shift $T e_j = \( w_(j + 1) \/ w_j \) e_(j + 1)$ on $K$ has invariant lattice ${ K_(gt.eq h) :
   h in bb(Z) union { - oo \, + oo } }$.]
] <lem:domar>

The convex-envelope and support-separation argument below follows the method of Domar’s weighted-translation work
  #cite(label("Domar1981")) Section 3. We prove the stated quadratic specialization in full,
  including the normalized estimates for both convolution tails.
  The signed exponent for a negative index is essential: the corresponding summand is $w_(- n)^(- 1 \/ n)$ for $n > 0$.

#proof[
