== 3 The 2-adic weights <sec:fibers>

We now construct the measurable family of shifts. Their logarithmic weights must satisfy the criterion of
  Section 2 while changing in opposite directions at the two ends when the base moves by one.

=== 3.1 The base transformation <the-base-transformation>

For the inverse-limit base $X = bb(Z)_2$ fixed in the introduction and $m gt.eq 1$, let

$ N_m = 2^m \, wide T_m \( x \) = x med mod med N_m in { 0 \, dots.h \, N_m - 1 } . $

Thus each residue is uniformly distributed. The map $theta x = x +
  1$ is an invertible probability-preserving transformation, and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(15)")
 $ T_(m + 1) \( x \) - T_m \( x \) & in { 0 \, N_m } \,\
T_m \( theta x \) - T_m \( x \) & =
  cases(delim: "{", 1 \, & T_m \( x \) < N_m - 1 \,, - \( N_m - 1 \) \, & T_m \( x \) = N_m - 1 .)
  $ <eq:residue-nesting>]

The finite residue classes generate the Borel sigma-algebra of $X$.

#thm([Lemma 3.1.])[
  #emph[The transformation $theta$ is ergodic.]
] <lem:ergodicity>

#proof[
  If $E$ is invariant modulo null sets, $theta$ cyclically permutes the $N_m$ residue classes, so
  $ mu \( E ∩ { T_m = r } \) = frac(mu \( E \), N_m) wide \( 0 lt.eq r < N_m \) . $
  Thus $E$ is independent of every finite union of residue classes.
    By the Monotone Class Theorem it is independent of every measurable set, in particular itself.
    Hence $mu \( E \) = mu \( E \)^2$. ◻
]

=== 3.2 The weights <the-weights>

Fix

$ c = 10 \, wide D = 100 \, wide ell_0 = 1 \, quad ell_m = 8^m med \( m gt.eq 1 \) . $

The integer blocks $I_m = { k : ell_(m - 1) lt.eq k < ell_m }$ partition the positive integers.
  Set $J_1 = 0$ and $J_(m + 1) = J_m + D N_m$; equivalently $J_m = D \( N_m - 2 \)$.
  Define $a_0^(+) \( x \) = a_0^(-) \( x \) = c$, and for $k in I_m$ put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(16)")
 $ a_k^(+) \( x \) & = c \( k + 1 \) + J_m + D T_m \( x \) \,\
a_k^(-) \( x \) & = c \( k + 1 \) + J_m + D \( N_m - 1 - T_m \( x \) \) . $ <eq:a-plus>]

On the full integer axis define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(17)")
 $ a_j \( x \) = cases(delim: "{", a_j^(+) \( x \) \, & j gt.eq 0 \,, a_(- j - 1)^(-) \( x \) \, & j < 0 \,) wide
   beta_j \( x \) = e^(- a_j \( x \)) . $ <eq:bilateral-a>]

The two central weights satisfy $beta_(- 1) \( x \) = beta_0 \( x \) = e^(- c)$ for every $x$.
  The linear terms in $a_k^plus.minus$ give quadratic growth of the cumulative logarithms.
  The smaller residue corrections change by $+ D$ and $- D$ under $theta$ unless the residue wraps around;
  the increments of $J_m$ preserve monotonicity across block boundaries.
  We first check the invariant-subspace criterion, then use those opposite changes to construct the backward
  intertwiner.

#thm([Proposition 3.2.])[
  #emph[For every $x in X$, the operator $S_x e_j = beta_j \( x \) e_(j + 1)$ is bounded, has norm $e^(- c)$,
    and has precisely the standard tails as its closed invariant subspaces.
    For each $v in K$, the map $x mapsto S_x v$ is measurable.]
] <prop:fibers>

#proof[
  We verify positivity, regularity, and growth separately. The corrections to $c \( k + 1 \)$ in (16)–(16) are
    nonnegative. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(18)")
   $ 0 < beta_j \( x \) lt.eq e^(- c) \, wide sum_(j in bb(Z)) beta_j \( x \) lt.eq
     2 sum_(k gt.eq 0) e^(- c \( k + 1 \)) < oo . $ <eq:beta-sum>]
  Within a block, both $a_k^plus.minus$ increase by $c$ at each step.
    At a boundary from $I_m$ to $I_(m + 1)$, put $delta = T_(m + 1) \( x \) - T_m \( x \) in { 0 \, N_m }$.
    The increments are, respectively,
  $ c + D N_m + D delta gt.eq c \, wide c + D \( N_(m + 1) - delta \) gt.eq c . $
  Also $a_1^plus.minus gt.eq 2 c > a_0^plus.minus$. Consequently $beta_j$ is nonincreasing for $j gt.eq
    0$ and nondecreasing for $j lt.eq 0$, including the equality at $- 1 \, 0$.
  Set $w_0 \( x \) = 1$ and define $w_(j + 1) \( x \) \/ w_j \( x \) = beta_j \( x \)$ for all $j in bb(Z)$.
    This gives a positive decreasing sequence. Since $log w_(j + 1) - log w_j = log beta_j$,
    its logarithm has the required convexity and concavity.
  For $k in I_m$ we have $N_m lt.eq 2 k^(1 \/ 3)$, and hence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(19)")
   $ 0 lt.eq a_k^plus.minus \( x \) - c \( k + 1 \) lt.eq 4 D k^(1 \/ 3) wide \( k gt.eq 1 \) . $ <eq:a-error>]
  The bound is uniform in $x$. Summing gives, as $n arrow.r oo$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(20)")
   $ log w_n \( x \) & = - sum_(k = 0)^(n - 1) a_k^(+) \( x \) = - frac(c n \( n + 1 \), 2) + O \( n^(4 \/ 3) \) \,\
log w_(- n) \( x \) & = sum_(k = 0)^(n - 1) a_k^(-) \( x \) = frac(c n \( n + 1 \), 2) +
  O \( n^(4 \/ 3) \) . $ <eq:w-positive>]
  Thus (3) holds with $a = c \/ 2 = 5 > log 3$. Moreover the lower bounds $a_k^plus.minus gt.eq c \( k + 1 \)$ imply
  $ w_n \( x \)^(1 \/ n) lt.eq e^(- c \( n + 1 \) \/ 2) \, wide w_(- n) \( x \)^(- 1 \/ n) lt.eq
    e^(- c \( n + 1 \) \/ 2) wide \( n gt.eq 1 \) . $
  These bounds prove (2). Lemma 3 therefore applies to every fiber.
  The norm statement follows from (18) and $beta_0 = e^(- c)$.
    Each coefficient $beta_j$ is measurable, so $x mapsto S_x e_j$ is measurable.
    Finite basis expansions and the uniform norm bound give strong measurability on every vector of $K$. ◻
]

We may now define $S in cal(B) \( H_0 \)$ by $\( S f \) \( x \) = S_x f \( x \)$. It has norm $e^(- c)$.
  For each $g in L^oo \( X \)$, the scalar multiplier $\( M_g f \) \( x \) =
  g \( x \) f \( x \)$ commutes with $S$. Before constructing the operator that links different fibers and moves
  their tails backward, we record the uniform power bound for $S$.

#thm([Proposition 3.3.])[
  #emph[The operator $S$ is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent. More precisely, for all integers $n gt.eq 1$,
$ parallel S^n parallel lt.eq exp (- c ⌊frac(\( n + 1 \)^2, 4)⌋) . $

]
] <prop:normquasi>

#proof[
  Define $d \( j \) = j + 1$ for $j gt.eq 0$ and $d \( j \) = - j$ for $j < 0$.
    The weight definitions imply $a_j \( x \) gt.eq c d \( j \)$ for every $x \, j$. Since
  $ S_x^n e_j = exp (- sum_(r = 0)^(n - 1) a_(j + r) \( x \)) e_(j + n) \, $
  the norm of each fiber power is bounded by the claimed quantity.
    Indeed the minimum of $sum_(r = 0)^(n - 1) d \( j + r \)$ over $j in
    bb(Z)$ is attained by an interval centered as closely as possible on the two minima at $- 1$ and $0$.
    For $n = 2 m$ this minimum is $m \( m + 1 \)$; for $n = 2 m + 1$ it is $\( m + 1 \)^2$.
    Both values are $floor.l \( n + 1 \)^2 \/ 4 floor.r$. The bound is independent of $x$,
    so integrating the fiber estimate proves it for $S^n$ itself.
    Consequently $lim_(n arrow.r oo) parallel S^n parallel^(1 \/ n) = 0$.
    Finally $S \( 1 ⊗ e_0 \) = e^(- c) \( 1 ⊗ e_1 \) eq.not 0$. ◻
]

== 4 A bounded backward intertwiner <sec:intertwiner>

We seek an operator which sends $e_j$ in the fiber at $x$ to a positive multiple of $e_(j - 1)$ in the fiber at
  $theta x$. Commutation with the forward shift determines a recurrence for those multiples.
  The opposite logarithmic drifts at the two ends of the fiber make its solutions bounded after normalization.

=== 4.1 Two-ended drift <two-ended-drift>

Write $Delta a_j \( x \) = a_j \( theta x \) - a_j \( x \)$.
  A block $I_m$ has an exceptional residue at $x$ when $T_m \( x \) = N_m - 1$. Since

$ sum_(m gt.eq 1) mu { T_m = N_m - 1 } = sum_(m gt.eq 1) 2^(- m) < oo \, $

the Borel–Cantelli Lemma shows that almost every $x$ has only finitely many such blocks.
  Intersect this conull set over all iterates of $theta$ to obtain a measurable $theta$-invariant conull set
  $X_(*)$. No independence of the exceptional events is needed.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  #emph[For every $x in X_(*)$ there is a finite $C \( x \)$ such that, for all $n \, q gt.eq 1$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(21)")
 $ sum_(k = 0)^(n - 1) Delta a_k \( x \) & gt.eq D n - C \( x \) \,\
sum_(k = - q)^(- 1) Delta a_k \( x \) & lt.eq - D q + C \( x \) . $ <eq:positive-drift>]

]
] <lem:drift>

#proof[
  For $k in I_m$, Equation (15) gives
  $ Delta a_k^(+) \( x \) = D - D N_m upright(bold(1))_({ T_m = N_m - 1 }) \( x \) \, wide
    Delta a_k^(-) \( x \) = - Delta a_k^(+) \( x \) . $
  Both central increments $Delta a_0^plus.minus$ vanish. Thus the first sum is
  $ D \( n - 1 \) - D sum_(m gt.eq 1\
T_m \( x \) = N_m - 1) N_m \| I_m ∩ \[ 1 \, n \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] . $
  Under $k mapsto - k - 1$, the negative-index sum is the negative of the same expression with $n = q$.
    Both claims follow on taking
  $ C \( x \) = D + D sum_(m gt.eq 1\
T_m \( x \) = N_m - 1) N_m \| I_m \| < oo . $
  ◻
]

The constant $C \( x \)$ may be unbounded and nonintegrable as a function of $x$.
  Only its pointwise finiteness is used below.

=== 4.2 Solving and normalizing the recurrence <solving-and-normalizing-the-recurrence>

For positive coefficients $b_j \( x \)$, sending $e_j$ at $x$ to $b_j \( x \) e_(j - 1)$ at $theta
  x$ would commute with $S$ precisely when

$ b_j \( x \) beta_(j - 1) \( theta x \) = beta_j \( x \) b_(j + 1) \( x \) . $

Figure 1 shows the two paths in this coefficient identity.
  The left side is obtained by moving backward and then forward, and the right side in the opposite order.

#figure(
  [#cetz.canvas({
  import cetz.draw: *
  content((0, 2), [#text(size: 9pt)[$e_j "at" x$]])
  content((6, 2), [#text(size: 9pt)[$e_(j+1) "at" x$]])
  content((0, 0), [#text(size: 9pt)[$e_(j-1) "at" theta x$]])
  content((6, 0), [#text(size: 9pt)[$e_j "at" theta x$]])
  line((0.5453, 2), (5.344, 2), stroke: (thickness: 0.4pt, miter-limit: 10), mark: (end: (symbol: ">>", fill: black)))
  content((3, 2), [#text(size: 9pt)[$S_x:#h(0.3333em) beta_j(x)$]], anchor: "south", padding: 0.1176cm)
  line((0.7596, 0), (5.351, 0), stroke: (thickness: 0.4pt, miter-limit: 10), mark: (end: (symbol: ">>", fill: black)))
  content((3, 0), [#text(size: 9pt)[$S_( theta x):#h(0.3333em) beta_(j-1)( theta x)$]], anchor: "north", padding: 0.1176cm)
  line((0, 1.732), (0, 0.2802), stroke: (thickness: 0.4pt, miter-limit: 10), mark: (end: (symbol: ">>", fill: black)))
  content((0, 1), [#text(size: 9pt)[$V_x:#h(0.3333em) b_j(x)$]], anchor: "east", padding: 0.1176cm)
  line((6, 1.732), (6, 0.2802), stroke: (thickness: 0.4pt, miter-limit: 10), mark: (end: (symbol: ">>", fill: black)))
  content((6, 1), [#text(size: 9pt)[$V_x:#h(0.3333em) b_(j+1)(x)$]], anchor: "west", padding: 0.1176cm)
})],
  caption: [Figure 1: The coefficient identity at a fixed input fiber. Both paths take $e_j$ at $x$ to a multiple of $e_j$ at $theta x$. The horizontal maps move forward in the coordinate, while the vertical maps move backward and translate the base. ],
) <fig:square>

We first solve this equation with initial value one, then divide all coefficients in each fiber by a common bound.
  For every $x in X$ set $tilde(b)_0 \( x \) = 1$ and define positive coefficients at all integer indices by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(22)")
 $ tilde(b)_(j + 1) \( x \) = tilde(b)_j \( x \) frac(beta_(j - 1) \( theta x \), beta_j \( x \))
   wide \( j in bb(Z) \) . $ <eq:b-recursion>]

Every coefficient is a finite product of positive measurable functions and their reciprocals,
  so it is finite and measurable.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  #emph[For $x in X_(*)$, the coefficients $tilde(b)_j \( x \)$ tend to zero as $j arrow.r +
    oo$ and as $j arrow.r - oo$. Consequently there are measurable functions $b_j :
    X arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ which are positive on $X_(*)$ and satisfy
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(23)")
 $ b_(j + 1) \( x \) beta_j \( x \) = b_j \( x \) beta_(j - 1) \( theta x \) wide \( x in X \, med j in bb(Z) \) .
   $ <eq:commuting-coefficients>]

]
] <lem:normalization>

#proof[
  Taking logarithms in (22) and summing forward gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(24)")
   $ log tilde(b)_n \( x \) & = sum_(k = 0)^(n - 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a_k \( x \) -
     a_(k - 1) \( theta x \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]\
 & = - sum_(k = 0)^(n - 1) Delta a_k \( x \) + a_(n - 1) \( theta x \) - a_(- 1) \( theta x \) \, wide n gt.eq
   1 . $ <eq:b-positive>]
  Summing backward from index $0$ gives the different sign
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(25)")
   $ log tilde(b)_(- q) \( x \) & = - sum_(k = - q)^(- 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a_k \( x \) -
     a_(k - 1) \( theta x \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]\
 & = sum_(k = - q)^(- 1) Delta a_k \( x \) + a_(- q - 1) \( theta x \) - a_(- 1) \( theta x \) \, wide q gt.eq
   1 . $ <eq:b-negative>]
  Here $a_(- 1) = c$. The boundary indices have different forms:
    $a_(n - 1) = a_(n - 1)^(+)$, whereas $a_(- q - 1) = a_q^(-)$.
    Using $a_0^plus.minus = c$ and Equation (19), we obtain
  $ a_(n - 1) \( theta x \) - c lt.eq c \( n - 1 \) + 4 D \( n - 1 \)^(1 \/ 3) \, wide a_(- q - 1) \( theta x \) -
    c lt.eq c q + 4 D q^(1 \/ 3) . $
  Combining these bounds with Lemma 7, we obtain the explicit estimates
  $ log tilde(b)_n \( x \) & lt.eq - \( D - c \) n + C \( x \) - c + 4 D \( n - 1 \)^(1 \/ 3) \,\
log tilde(b)_(- q) \( x \) & lt.eq - \( D - c \) q + C \( x \) + 4 D q^(1 \/ 3) . $
  Since $D - c = 90 > 0$, both right sides tend to $- oo$. These estimates are pointwise in $x$;
    they do not require an integrable bound on $C \( x \)$.
  The countable supremum
  $ L \( x \) = sup_(j in bb(Z)) tilde(b)_j \( x \) $
  is measurable, and $1 lt.eq L \( x \) < oo$ on $X_(*)$. Define
  $ b_j \( x \) = cases(delim: "{", tilde(b)_j \( x \) \/ L \( x \) \, & x in X_(*) \,, 0 \, & x in.not X_(*) .) $
  All indices at a fixed $x$ are divided by the same number, so (22) yields (23).
    Off $X_(*)$ both sides of that identity vanish. ◻
]

Define a backward shift $V_x : K arrow.r K$ by $V_x e_j = b_j \( x \) e_(j - 1)$.
  Every $V_x$ has norm at most one, and $x mapsto V_x v$ is measurable for every $v in
  K$ by finite-coordinate approximation. It defines $V in cal(B) \( H_0 \)$ by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(26)")
 $ \( V f \) \( theta x \) = V_x f \( x \) \, wide upright("equivalently ") \( V f \) \( theta x \, j - 1 \) =
   b_j \( x \) f \( x \, j \) . $ <eq:V-definition>]

Invariance of $mu$ gives $lr(bar.v.double V f bar.v.double)^2 =
  integral_X lr(bar.v.double V_x f \( x \) bar.v.double)^2 thin d mu \( x \) lt.eq lr(bar.v.double f bar.v.double)^2$.

#thm([Proposition 4.3.])[
  #emph[The contraction $V$ commutes with $S$. For every $x in X_(*)$ and $h in bb(Z)$,
$ overline(V_x K_(gt.eq h)) = K_(gt.eq h - 1) \, wide overline(V_x K) = K . $

]
] <prop:V>

#proof[
  At the same output base point $theta x$, the two compositions have coefficients
  $ S_(theta x) V_x e_j = b_j \( x \) beta_(j - 1) \( theta x \) e_j \, wide V_x S_x e_j =
    beta_j \( x \) b_(j + 1) \( x \) e_j . $
  They agree by (23), first on the basis and then on $K$. Equation (26) now gives $S V = V S$.
    On $X_(*)$ every $b_j \( x \) > 0$. The image of a tail therefore contains every basis vector in the preceding
    tail, up to a nonzero scalar, and is contained in that preceding tail. This proves the density assertions. ◻
]

The density conclusion, rather than surjectivity, is exactly what is needed for closed invariant subspaces.
  No bounded inverse of $V_x$ or $V$ is asserted.

== 5 All common invariant subspaces <sec:subspaces>

We now show that the scalar multipliers, $S$, and $V$ have no proper nonzero closed common invariant subspace.
  The first step accounts for every closed subspace invariant under the multipliers,
  without assuming in advance that it is defined fiber by fiber.

=== 5.1 Multipliers force a measurable field of projections <multipliers-force-a-measurable-field-of-projections>

Let $M subset.eq H_0$ be a closed subspace invariant under $S$, $V$, and every scalar multiplier.
  Since $M_(macron(g)) = M_g^(*)$ belongs to the same multiplier family, $M$ reduces each multiplier.
  Its orthogonal projection $P$ therefore commutes with all of them.
  The field lemma #cite(label("CircleCompanion")) Lemma 3.1,
  applied with the probability space $\( X \, mu \)$ and $K = ell^2 \( bb(Z) \)$, gives

$ \( P f \) \( x \) = P_x f \( x \) \, wide M = { f in H_0 :
  f \( x \) in "ran" P_x upright(" for almost every ") x } \, $

where the $P_x$ are orthogonal projections outside one null set and $x mapsto P_x v$ is measurable for each $v in
  K$. These are exactly the projection and measurability conclusions needed below;
  no covariance property is imposed on this field.

=== 5.2 Fiber invariance and measurable tail labels <fiber-invariance-and-measurable-tail-labels>

For this fixed subspace $M$ and its projection field $P_x$, the global invariance identities $\( I - P \) S P =
  0$ and $\( I - P \) V P = 0$ imply

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(27)")
 $ \( I - P_x \) S_x P_x & = 0 \,\
\( I - P_(theta x) \) V_x P_x & = 0 $ <eq:fiber-S>]

almost everywhere. To justify the passage, apply each global identity to the constant field $e_j$.
  In the second identity evaluate the output at $theta x$ and use probability preservation.
  The resulting equalities hold for every $j$ on a common conull set and extend to all of $K$ by boundedness.
  Intersect this set, the projection set, and $X_(*)$ over all integer iterates of $theta$.
  We can henceforth use all these identities on a single invariant conull set.
  This set is chosen after fixing $M$; no intersection over all candidate subspaces is required.

By Proposition 5 and (27), there is a label

$ s \( x \) in bb(Z) union { - oo \, + oo } \, wide "ran" P_x = K_(gt.eq s \( x \)) . $

It is measurable: for every $j in bb(Z)$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(28)")
 $ { x : s \( x \) lt.eq j } = { x : chevron.l P_x e_j \, e_j chevron.r = 1 } $ <eq:label-measurable>]

on the conull set, and the label can be set to $+ oo$ elsewhere. Equation (27) and Proposition 9 give two implications:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(29)")
 $ s \( x \) = - oo & med arrow.r.double med s \( theta x \) = - oo \,\
s \( x \) in bb(Z) & med arrow.r.double med s \( theta x \) lt.eq s \( x \) - 1 . $ <eq:full-descent>]

The first uses $overline(V_x K) = K$. The second uses $overline(V_x K_(gt.eq h)) =
  K_(gt.eq h - 1)$ and the inclusion $K_(gt.eq h - 1) subset.eq "ran" P_(theta x)$.
  When the input fiber is zero, there is no restriction on the output label.

=== 5.3 Strict descent on a probability space <strict-descent-on-a-probability-space>

The final measure argument is useful independently of the construction.

#thm([Lemma 5.1.])[
  #emph[Let $theta$ be an invertible ergodic probability-preserving transformation of $\( Y \, nu \)$.
    Suppose a measurable function $s : Y arrow.r bb(Z) union { - oo \, + oo }$ satisfies, almost everywhere,
$ s \( x \) = - oo med arrow.r.double med s \( theta x \) = - oo \, wide s \( x \) in bb(Z) med arrow.r.double med
  s \( theta x \) lt.eq s \( x \) - 1 . $

 Then $s = - oo$ almost everywhere or $s = + oo$ almost everywhere.]
] <lem:descent>

#proof[
  All inclusions in this proof are modulo null sets. Put $E = { s = - oo }$.
    The first implication gives $theta E subset.eq E$. Probability preservation makes this inclusion an equality
    modulo null sets, so ergodicity gives $nu \( E \) in { 0 \, 1 }$. The case $nu \( E \) = 1$ is one conclusion.
  Suppose $nu \( E \) = 0$. For each integer $j$, put $F_j = { s lt.eq j }$. Both implications give
  $ theta F_j subset.eq F_(j - 1) subset.eq F_j . $
  It follows that $nu \( F_(j - 1) \) = nu \( F_j \)$. For any fixed $j$, continuity from above now gives
  $ nu \( F_j \) = nu (⋂_(r gt.eq 0) F_(j - r)) = nu \( E \) = 0 . $
  The countable union of the $F_j$ is ${ s < + oo }$, proving the other conclusion. ◻
]

Applying Lemma 10 shows that $P_x = I$ almost everywhere or $P_x = 0$ almost everywhere.
  We have therefore proved that the unital complex algebra generated by $S$, $V$,
  and all scalar multipliers is transitive.

== 6 The commutant and structural consequences <sec:structure>

#proof[
  Proposition 5 proves the fiber classification and norm statement in Theorem 1,
    and Proposition 6 gives the uniform power bound and nonzeroness.
    Proposition 9 constructs the commuting contraction with its dense tail images.
    Section 5 proves transitivity of the algebra generated by $S$, $V$, and the scalar multipliers.
    These establish all assertions of the theorem.
  That transitive algebra is contained in ${ S }'$, so any closed ${
    S }'$-invariant subspace is zero or all of $H_0$. The nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator $S$ is nonscalar.
    The commutant lemma #cite(label("CircleCompanion")) Lemma 1.2,
    with this $S$ on the complex Hilbert space $H_0$, now implies that ${
    S }'$ is a proper strongly closed unital complex algebra. Together with the lattice conclusion, it is transitive.
  Finally, the finite residue classes form a countable generating algebra on $X$,
    so $L^2 \( X \)$ and $H_0$ are separable. The space $H_0$ is infinite-dimensional.
    Every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space is unitarily isomorphic to $H_0$;
    conjugation by such a unitary transfers $S$, its norm estimates, its commutant,
    and the invariant-subspace conclusions. This proves the corollary. ◻
]

=== 6.1 Ordinary invariant subspaces and compactness <ordinary-invariant-subspaces-and-compactness>

For every $h in bb(Z)$, the subspace $L^2 \( X \; K_(gt.eq h) \)$ is closed, nonzero, proper, and $S$-invariant.
  It is not invariant under $V$: the image of the constant field $e_h$ has its nonzero coordinate at $h -
  1$ almost everywhere. Thus the construction separates ordinary invariance from hyperinvariance in an explicit way.

Each fiber $S_x$ is compact. Indeed $a_k^plus.minus gt.eq c \( k + 1 \)$ implies $beta_j \( x \) arrow.r
  0$ as $\| j \| arrow.r oo$, and cutting off all but finitely many shift weights approximates $S_x$ in operator
  norm. The operator $S$ on $H_0$ is not compact. There is an infinite orthonormal sequence $\( phi_n \)$ in
  $L^2 \( X \)$, since the $N_m$ disjoint residue classes give $N_m$ orthogonal indicators for every $m$.
  The orthonormal vectors $phi_n ⊗ e_0$ have images

$ S \( phi_n ⊗ e_0 \) = e^(- c) phi_n ⊗ e_1 . $

These images are orthogonal with a common positive norm and have no norm-convergent subsequence.

=== 6.2 The multiplier algebra has infinite multiplicity <the-multiplier-algebra-has-infinite-multiplicity>

Let $Q_0$ be the projection onto $L^2 \( X \) ⊗ bb(C) e_0$.
  This gives a concrete witness to the properness of the commutant: on $1 ⊗ e_0$,

$ Q_0 S \( 1 ⊗ e_0 \) = 0 \, wide S Q_0 \( 1 ⊗ e_0 \) = e^(- c) \( 1 ⊗ e_1 \) \, $

so $Q_0 in.not { S }'$.

The same projection commutes with every scalar multiplier, but is not itself a scalar multiplier.
  If $Q_0 = M_g$, its action on $1 ⊗ e_0$ would give $g = 1$ almost everywhere,
  whereas its action on $1 ⊗ e_1$ is zero. The abelian self-adjoint algebra ${ M_g :
  g in L^oo \( X \) }$ is therefore not maximal abelian in $cal(B) \( H_0 \)$.
  Arveson’s maximal-abelian inclusion theorem #cite(label("Arveson1967")) Theorem 3.3 does not apply to this
  subalgebra of the transitive commutant.

=== 6.3 Atomic finite families and a diffuse joint algebra <atomic-finite-families-and-a-diffuse-joint-algebra>

We finish with a spectral property of the constructed weights. This subsection is independent of the transitivity proof.

Let $\| S \| = \( S^(*) S \)^(1 \/ 2)$. Identify $H_0$ with $L^2 \( X times bb(Z) \, mu times \# \)$,
  where $\#$ is counting measure, and let $U e_j = e_(j + 1)$ on every fiber. Then

$ S = U \| S \| \, wide \( \| S \| f \) \( x \, j \) = beta_j \( x \) f \( x \, j \) . $

All weights are positive, so $\| S \|$ is injective with dense range and $U$ is the unitary polar part of $S$. Define

$ A_n = U^(- n) \| S \| U^n quad \( n in bb(Z) \) \; wide \( A_n f \) \( x \, j \) =
  beta_(j + n) \( x \) f \( x \, j \) . $

For a family of operators, $W^(*)$ denotes the smallest strongly closed unital self-adjoint algebra containing
  that family. A nonzero projection in an abelian von Neumann algebra is minimal if it contains no smaller nonzero
  projection in that algebra. Such an algebra is #emph[atomic] when its identity is the strong sum of its minimal
  projections, and #emph[diffuse] when it has no minimal projection.
  We will show that $W^(*) \( A_n : n in F \)$ is atomic for every finite $F subset bb(Z)$,
  whereas $W^(*) \( A_n : n in bb(Z) \)$ is diffuse.

For any finite $F subset bb(Z)$ and fixed $j$, the tuple $\( beta_(j + n) \( x \) \)_(n in F)$ depends on finitely
  many residues $T_m \( x \)$. Because the residues are nested,
  it depends on one residue $T_M \( x \)$ and takes finitely many values.
  Allowing the countably many values of $j$ gives a countable range for the joint tuple on $X times bb(Z)$.
  Its nonempty level sets have positive measure: on every fixed coordinate slice they are finite unions of residue
  classes. These level sets give the minimal projections of the generated abelian algebra,
  and their sum is the identity. Thus $W^(*) \( A_n : n in F \)$ is atomic.
  In particular, $\| S \|$ has a pure-point spectral resolution.

For the full family, the two equal central weights determine the coordinate slice.
  They are the only weights equal to $e^(- c)$, since $a_k^plus.minus gt.eq c \( k + 1 \) > c$ for every $k gt.eq
  1$. At $\( x \, j \)$ the tuple indices $n$ for which $beta_(j + n) \( x \) = e^(- c)$ are therefore exactly $-
  j - 1$ and $- j$. For every $r in bb(Z)$, the projection onto the slice $j = r$, denoted by $P_r$,
  belongs to $W^(*) \( A_n : n in bb(Z) \)$: it is

$ upright(bold(1))_({ e^(- c) }) \( A_(- r - 1) \) thin upright(bold(1))_({ e^(- c) }) \( A_(- r) \) . $

Next fix $m gt.eq 1$ and choose $k_m = ell_(m - 1) in I_m$.
  On the slice $j = r$, the multiplier $A_(k_m - r)$ supplies $beta_(k_m) \( x \)$. For $0 lt.eq t < N_m$, put

$ lambda_(m \, t) = exp \[ - c \( k_m + 1 \) - J_m - D t \] . $

Equation (16) shows that the multiplication projection of the residue rectangle ${ T_m = t } times { r }$ is

$ P_r thin upright(bold(1))_({ lambda_(m \, t) }) \( A_(k_m - r) \) . $

Thus every residue-class projection on every coordinate slice belongs to the generated algebra.
  Such rectangles generate the product sigma-algebra. Conversely all $A_n$ are multiplication operators, so,
  modulo completion and null sets,

$ W^(*) \( A_n : n in bb(Z) \) = L^oo \( X times bb(Z) \, mu times \# \) $

in its multiplication representation. Haar measure on $bb(Z)_2$ has no atoms, and consequently this algebra is diffuse.

The pure-point assertion does not assert discreteness of the spectrum or compactness of $S$.
  Nor is this full multiplier algebra the scalar base algebra used in the transitivity proof.
  In fact $\| S \|$ does not commute with $S$, because the adjacent weights $beta_0 = e^(- c)$ and $beta_1 lt.eq
  e^(- 2 c)$ differ. The polar-conjugate algebra is therefore not contained in ${ S }'$.

